! C22-6578 PROBLEM 3.9, "DOUBLE-PRECISION COMPARE", printed pp.32-34
! (PDF 33-35), read in the ink.
!
!   "The accumulator contains a double precision floating-point
!   quantity.  Another double precision floating-point quantity is
!   stored in two full words, with the more significant part in M1, less
!   significant part in M1+1.  Compare the two double precision
!   quantities and set the appropriate indicators $AE, $AL and $AH."
!
!   Method 2.  Compare high order parts.  If they compare "equal,"
!   perform the double precision subtraction to ascertain the result.
!       DKOMP2  ST(U), A1               'save accumulator
!               SLO(U), A1+1.
!               K(U), M                 'single precision compare
!               BAE, DPSUB              'usually unsuccessful
!       END     BEW, $                  'end of program
!       DPSUB   DL(U), A1+1.            'full-scale double precision subtract
!               D-(U), M1+1.
!               D+(N), A1
!               D-(N), M1
!               L(BU,3), $RLZ           '$RLZ, $RZ, $RGZ fetched
!               ST(BU,3), $AL           $AL, $AE, $AH stored
!               DL(U), A1+1.            'restore accumulator
!               D+(U), A1
!               B, END
!       A1      DR(U), (2)
!
! UNCERTAIN READINGS AND THE PAGE'S OWN SLIPS, kept as printed:
!   "K(U), M": the problem names the location M1, and nothing is called
!     M.  The deck supplies "M SYN, M1" with its data, the one statement
!     needed for it to assemble; the compare is of the high-order parts,
!     as the page's own description says.
!   "D-(U), M1+1." here against "D-(N), M1+1." in Method 1; the period
!     after "M1+1" is printed like a small "L".  Read as M1+1. (U).
!   The comment on ST(BU,3) has no opening quote mark in the ink.
! The printed statements follow the deck's own two, which load the
! accumulator, and come before the deck's data, as printed: spacing,
! trailing periods and all.  The page defines neither M1 nor the
! accumulator's value; the deck's are below.
!
! ASSEMBLED WITH ASM=strap2 (see problem_3_1.strap; "SLO (U)" with its
! space assembles as SLO(U)).
!
! WHAT THE PROGRAM DOES, with the page of the rule:
!   ST(U), A1 and SLO(U), A1+1. save the accumulator as two words:
!     STORE, Reference Manual printed p.90; STORE LOW ORDER, p.97, "The
!     exponent is obtained by subtracting 48 from the exponent in bits
!     0-11 of the accumulator", the fraction is accumulator bits 60-107.
!   The double-precision subtraction is Figure 28's (p.97), the order of
!     its notes: low parts first, then add A1, then subtract M1.  ADD
!     DOUBLE, p.95; LOAD DOUBLE, p.96.  The last D-(N) sets RLZ, RZ or
!     RGZ "as in ADD" on the 96-bit difference.
!   L(BU, 3), $RLZ fetches indicator bits 56-58 (RLZ, RZ, RGZ) into the
!     accumulator's right end, offset 0: accumulator bits 125-127.
!   ST(BU, 3), $AL stores them into bits 60-62 (AL, AE, AH).
!   DL(U), A1+1. / D+(U), A1 restore the accumulator.  LOAD DOUBLE:
!     "Accumulator bits 60-107 are set to zero.  Accumulator bits 108-127
!     remain unchanged" (p.96); ADD DOUBLE: "Accumulator bits 108-127
!     remain unchanged" (p.95).  So the THREE BITS THE PROGRAM FETCHED
!     STAY IN ACCUMULATOR BITS 125-127, word 9's last three bits, and
!     the deck checks them there: 4 = 100 = less, 2 = 010 = equal,
!     1 = 001 = greater.
!   METHOD 2'S COMPARE, K(U), M (p.90-91): "When the exponent
!     difference between the operands exceeds 48, the algebraically
!     larger of the two numbers, judged by exponent and fraction sign,
!     is considered high ... When the exponent difference is 48 or
!     less, a zero result is considered an equality."  Only on AE does
!     the program reach DPSUB; either way it ends at END (comment (b),
!     "convenient to end at the same place").  On the K path the
!     accumulator is untouched and word 9 keeps what the deck put there.
!
! FIXED 2026-11-16, AND THE INDICATOR WORD IS NOW CHECKED: a store into
! word 11 lands in its bits 20-63 (Reference Manual printed p.37,
! bsv/StretchStorage.bsv indStW).  The paragraph below is the record of
! the limitation.
! >>> (UNTIL 2026-11-16) THE COMPARISON INDICATORS THEMSELVES WERE NOT
! CHECKED: THE MODEL DROPPED THE STORE INTO THEM. <<<  ST(BU, 3), $AL changed nothing on
! the model (bsv/StretchStorage.bsv, writeWord: stores to word 11 are a
! documented SIMPLIFICATION, dropped silently).  Reproducer:
! decks/problem_3_vfl_store_to_indicators.strap; docs/NEXT.md
! "C22-6578 CHAPTER 3" item 6.  tests/run_tests.sh carries the
! indicator word's expected value as a comment, to be restored when the
! store is implemented.
!
! THIS DECK'S DATA AND RESULT, HAND-DERIVED (tests/problem_3_expected.py
! p39_run follows the same rules and agrees):
!   THE PAGE'S OWN COUNTER-EXAMPLE TO METHOD 2, comment (a) on p.34,
!   with E = 1:
!     accumulator   (E+1+48, 0)  and  (E+1, 0)
!     M1, M1+1      (E, .111...1)  and  (E-48, .111...1)
!   "Present program would lead to (A1, A1+1.) larger, because of the
!   48 unit difference in the exponent.  Correct answer should lead to
!   (M1, M1+1.) larger".  THE DECK CHECKS THE PAGE'S CLAIM ABOUT ITS OWN
!   PROGRAM: K(U) sees an exponent difference of 49, over 48, and the
!   positive number with the larger exponent is high: AH, and BAE does
!   not branch.  The order of the quantities is M larger; the program
!   says otherwise, as the page says it will.
!     A1         0640000000000000   (exponent 50, zero fraction)
!     A1+1.      0040000000000000   (exponent 2, zero fraction)
!     word 8     0640000000000000   (untouched on this path)
!     word 9     0000000000000000
!     indicator word 0000001000000042: AH (bit 62) set by K itself, so
!       this one IS checked.  Instruction by instruction: the deck's
!       DL(U) and D+(U) of zero fractions set RZ, and no LS since both
!       operands are zero (p.96); ST(U) and SLO(U) set MOP and the
!       result indicators "as in ADD TO MEMORY" (p.90, p.97): RZ again,
!       a zero fraction stored, MOP on; K(U) sets MOP and PSH "as in
!       ADD" (p.91): MOP off, PSH on for a difference over 48, and AH;
!       "The arithmetic result ... indicators ... are not changed"
!       (p.91), so RZ stays.  RZ bit 57, PSH bit 27, AH bit 62.
!     PC 70, END.
!
! INJECTION, OBSERVED 2026-11-16 (data side, on a copy of the deck, the
! deck itself untouched): AHI changed from 0.0X50 to 0.0X49, an exponent
! difference of exactly 48.  K now finds a zero difference, AE, and the
! program goes through DPSUB and finds M larger (verdict 4).  FAIL A1,
! A1+1, word 8 (the changed exponent), verdict_acc_right (got 4) and IND
! (got 0000002000000044); the PC passed (70: both paths end at END).

! --- the deck's own: load the accumulator with (AHI, ALO) ---
START   DL(U), AHI
        D+(U), ALO
! --- the printed program ---
DKOMP2  ST(U), A1
        SLO(U), A1+1.
        K(U), M
        BAE, DPSUB
END     BEW, $
DPSUB   DL(U), A1+1.
        D-(U), M1+1.
        D+(N), A1
        D-(N), M1
        L(BU,3), $RLZ
        ST(BU,3), $AL
        DL(U), A1+1.
        D+(U), A1
        B, END
A1      DR(U), (2)
! --- the deck's data ---
M       SYN, M1
M1      DD(N), 281474976710655X1
        DD(N), 281474976710655X-47
AHI     DD(N), 0.0X50
ALO     DD(N), 0.0X2
        END, START
